<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">9 November in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">5 events that happened on 9 November, from 694 to 1989.</p></header>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">694</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">The Seventeenth Council of Toledo orders the enslavement of Jews</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Accusing Jews of conspiracy, the council called by King Egica decreed the enslavement of Jews in Visigothic Spain and the removal of their children. It was among the harshest anti-Jewish measures of the early Middle Ages.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1799</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Napoleon's coup of 18 Brumaire topples the Directory</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Napoleon Bonaparte and his allies began a coup against the Directory, completed the next day. The new Consulate made Napoleon First Consul and effective ruler of France.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consul">Consul</a></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1918</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Wilhelm II's abdication is announced as Germany becomes a republic</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">With revolution spreading, Chancellor Max von Baden announced the Kaiser's abdication and Philipp Scheidemann proclaimed a republic in Berlin. Wilhelm fled to the Netherlands the next day.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/german-empire">German Empire</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philipp-scheidemann">Philipp Scheidemann</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-ii">Wilhelm II</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaiser">Kaiser</a></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1938</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Kristallnacht: Nazi pogroms sweep Germany and Austria</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Over two nights, Nazi mobs burned hundreds of synagogues, wrecked about 7,500 Jewish businesses and killed at least 91 people. About 30,000 Jewish men were sent to concentration camps.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1989</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">The Berlin Wall opens</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">After a confused announcement that East Germans could travel freely, crowds pressed the border crossings until guards opened the gates. The fall of the Wall led to German reunification within a year.</p></li></ol>
